{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-351132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨t(6t-4),\\,12{t}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(20,30)$ when $t=2,$ calculate the components of the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$r(t)=⟨${{field-1}}$,\\,3{t}^{4}-18\\,⟩$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨t(6t-4),\\,12{t}^{3}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int t(6t-4)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 12{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,3{t}^{4}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(2)=⟨20,30⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨2(2)^{3}-2(2)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,3(2)^{4}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨20,30⟩ & =⟨8+{C}_{1},48+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=12$ and ${C}_{2}=-18.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+12,\\,3{t}^{4}-18⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-351132" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351132" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2t^3-2t^2+12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-7217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨t(3t-2),\\,8{t}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(30,90)$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-7217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨t(3t-2),\\,8{t}^{3}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int t(3t-2)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 8{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,2{t}^{4}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=⟨30,90⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨(3)^{3}-(3)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,2(3)^{4}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨30,90⟩ & =⟨18+{C}_{1},162+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=12$ and ${C}_{2}=-72.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+12,\\,2{t}^{4}-72⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-7217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-7217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-7217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r=⟨{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+2,\\,{t}^{4}-24⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+12,\\,2{t}^{4}-72⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+2,\\,2{t}^{4}-24⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+2,\\,2{t}^{4}-42⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{4}-42⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-7217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+12,\\,2{t}^{4}-72⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-122422" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}},\\,-1⟩,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(5,2)$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-122422" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}},\\,-1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (-1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\sqrt{t+1}+{C}_{1},\\,-t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=⟨5,2⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨2\\sqrt{3+1}+{C}_{1},\\,-3+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨5,2⟩ & =⟨4+{C}_{1},-3+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=5.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t+1}+1,\\,5-t⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-122422" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-122422" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-122422" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t+1}+1,\\,11-3t⟩"] ["math" "r(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t+1}+1,\\,5-t⟩"] ["math" "r(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t+1}-1,\\,8-t⟩"] ["math" "r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t+1}+3,\\,5-t⟩"] ["math" "r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t+1}+3,\\,8-2t⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-122422" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t+1}+1,\\,5-t⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351145" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨4{e}^{-4t},\\,5{e}^{5t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(1,6)$ when $t=0,$ calculate the components of the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$r(t)=⟨\\,-{e}^{-4t}+2\\,,$\n{{field-1}}$⟩$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351145" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨4{e}^{-4t},\\,5{e}^{5t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 4{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 5{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{-4t}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{5t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨1,6⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨-{e}^{-4(0)}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{5(0)}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,6⟩ & =⟨-1+{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=2$ and ${C}_{2}=5.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨-{e}^{-4t}+2,\\,{e}^{5t}+5⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351145" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-351145" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351145" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e^{5t}+5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-51354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0,0)$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-51354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-1.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-51354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-51354" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-51354" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-51354" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-122424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨6t-1,{t}^{2}-1⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨10,-1⟩$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-122424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨6t-1,{t}^{2}-1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (6t-1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int ({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-t+{C}_{1},\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=⟨10,-1⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨3({3}^{2})-3+{C}_{1},\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}-3+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨10,-1⟩ & =⟨24+{C}_{1},6+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-14$ and ${C}_{2}=-7.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨3{t}^{2}-t-14,\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨3({1}^{2})-1-14,\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}-1-7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-12,-\\frac{23}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-122424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-122424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-122424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r(1)=⟨-12,-6⟩"] ["math" "r(1)=⟨-12,-\\frac{23}{3}⟩"] ["math" "r(1)=⟨6,-\\frac{23}{3}⟩"] ["math" "r(1)=⟨\\frac{4}{3},-6⟩"] ["math" "r(1)=⟨\\frac{4}{3},\\frac{1}{3}⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-122424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r(1)=⟨-12,-\\frac{23}{3}⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{2t},\\,\\frac{1}{3t}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨1,2⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=e.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$r(e)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{2t},\\,\\frac{1}{3t}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\frac{1}{3t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(1)=⟨1,2⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+{C}_{1},\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2⟩.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=e,$ we substitute $t=e$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(e) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}(1)+1,\\frac{1}{3}(1)+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-351151" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351151" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351151" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351151" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351156" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨6{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t}⟩,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨-1,\\,2⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=1.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$r(1)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351156" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨6{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 6{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{3}+{C}_{1},\\,\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨-1,2⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨2(0)^{3}+{C}_{1},\\,\\sqrt{(0)^{3}}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=-1$ and ${C}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2{t}^{3}-1,\\,\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}+2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2(1)^{3}-1,\\,\\sqrt{(1)^{3}}+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-1,\\,1+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351156" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-351156" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351156" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351156" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351156" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-83454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨0,0⟩$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-83454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-2$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ we substitute $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{1}{2}) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})-2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-83454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-83454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-83454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-2,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨-2\\pi ,\\pi ⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,\\pi ⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,0⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-83454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨-2,1⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-85611" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2{e}^{2t},\\,3{t}^{2}+2t⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨1,2⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-85611" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2{e}^{2t},\\,3{t}^{2}+2t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (3{t}^{2}+2t)\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{2t}+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨1,2⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{e}^{2(0)}+{C}_{1},\\,(0)^{3}+(0)^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{e}^{2t},\\,{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+2⟩.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨{e}^{2(1)},\\,(1)^{3}+(1)^{2}+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{2},\\,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-85611" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-85611" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-85611" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r(1)=⟨2{e}^{2},\\,4⟩"] ["math" "r(1)=⟨2{e}^{2},\\,5⟩"] ["math" "r(1)=⟨{e}^{2},\\,2⟩"] ["math" "r(1)=⟨{e}^{2},\\,4⟩"] ["math" "r(1)=⟨{e}^{2},\\,5⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-85611" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r(1)=⟨{e}^{2},\\,4⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t+1},\\,2t-5⟩,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨0,6⟩$ when $t=0,$ find the times when the particle intersects the $x$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the particle intersects the $x$-axis when $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle intersects the $x$-axis when its position is $r=⟨x,0⟩,$ where $x$ is arbitrary. So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{2}{t+1},\\,2t-5⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{2}{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (2t-5)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{2}-5t+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨0,6⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+{C}_{1},\\,{0}^{2}-5(0)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,6⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=6.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1),\\,{t}^{2}-5t+6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times when the particle intersects the $x$-axis, we solve for the times at which the $y$-coordinate of the position vector is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-5t+6 & =0 \\\\ (t-2)(t-3) & =0 \\\\ t & =2,\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the particle intersects the $x$-axis when $t=2$ and $t=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-351258" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351258" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351258" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351258" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2t-3,\\,3{t}^{2}-6t+2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨2,\\,0⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the times when the particle is at the origin. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the particle is at the origin when $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle is at the origin when its position is $r=⟨0,0⟩.$ So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2t-3,\\,3{t}^{2}-6t+2⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (2t-3)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (3{t}^{2}-6t+2)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-3t+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+2t+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨2,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0-0+{C}_{1},\\,0-0+0+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=2$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{2}-3t+2,\\,{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is at the origin, we solve the equation $r(t)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{t}^{2}-3t+2,\\,{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+2t⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-3t+2 & =(t-1)(t-2)=0\\,⇒\\,t=1,2, \\\\ {t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+2t & =t(t-1)(t-2)=0\\,⇒\\,t=0,1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $t$ that satisfy both equations are $t=1$ and $t=2.$ \n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the particle is at the origin when\n$t=1$ and $t=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-351261" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351261" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351261" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351261" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-347213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t),\\,2t-5⟩,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨0,6⟩$ when $t=0,$ find the times when the particle intersects the $x$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the particle intersects the $x$-axis when $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-347213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle intersects the $x$-axis when its position is $r=⟨x,0⟩,$ where $x$ is arbitrary. So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t),\\,2t-5⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (2t-5)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{2}-5t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨0,6⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{C}_{1},\\,{0}^{2}-5(0)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,6⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=6.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t),\\,{t}^{2}-5t+6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times  when the particle intersects the $x$-axis, we solve for the times at which the $y$-coordinate of the position vector is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-5t+6 & =0 \\\\ (t-2)(t-3) & =0 \\\\ t & =2,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the particle intersects the $x$-axis when $t=2$ and $t=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-347213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-347213" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-347213" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-347213" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-347213" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨8(1+2t)^{3},\\,4t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨1,\\,-2⟩$ when $t=0,$ find the times when the particle intersects the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe particle intersects the $x$-axis when $t=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle intersects the $x$-axis when its position is $r=⟨x,0⟩,$ where $x$ is arbitrary. So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨8(1+2t)^{3},\\,4t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 8(1+2t)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(1+2t)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,2{t}^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨1,-2⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨(1+0)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,2(0)^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-2.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(1+2t)^{4},\\,2{t}^{2}-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times when the particle intersects the $x$-axis, we solve for the times at which the $y$-coordinate of the position vector is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 2(t-1)(t+1) & =0 \\\\ t & =1,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t$ must be positive, we conclude that the particle intersects the $x$-axis when $t=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-351223" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351223" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-85616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨4t,3{t}^{2}-1⟩\\,,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position  $r=⟨-2,0⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the times when the particle is at the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-85616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle is at the origin when its position is $r=⟨0,0⟩.$ So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨4t,3{t}^{2}-1⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 4t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (3{t}^{2}-1)\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{3}-t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨-2,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨2(0)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,{0}^{3}-0+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-2$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2{t}^{2}-2,\\,{t}^{3}-t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is at the origin, we solve the equation $r(t)=⟨0,0⟩.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2}-2,\\,{t}^{3}-t⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}-2=2(t-1)(t+1) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 1, \\\\ {t}^{3}-t=t(t-1)(t+1) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 1,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions that satisfy both equations are $t=\\pm 1.$ Therefore, we conclude that the particle is at the origin  when $t=\\pm 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-85616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-85616" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-85616" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=-1,0"] ["math" "t=0,1"] ["math" "t=\\pm 1"] ["math" "t=\\pm 1,0"] ["text" "The particle is never at the origin"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-85616" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=\\pm 1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-7218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na=⟨0,5⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(3,-3)$ and has velocity $⟨1,0⟩$ when $t=2,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-7218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨0,5⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 0\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 5\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,5t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=⟨1,\\,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,5(2)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,10+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=-10.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨1,\\,5t-10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1,\\,5t-10⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (5t-10)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t+{C}_{3},\\,\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}-10t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(2)=⟨3,-3⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨2+{C}_{3},\\,\\frac{5(2)^{2}}{2}-10(2)+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,-3⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{3},\\,-10+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=1$ and ${C}_{4}=7.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨t+1,\\,\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}-10t+7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-7218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-7218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-7218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r=⟨-{t}^{2}+t+1,\\,\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}-10t+17⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨2t+4,\\,\\frac{5{t}^{2}+7}{2}-5t⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨t+1,\\,\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}-10t+7⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨t+4,\\,\\frac{5{t}^{2}+7}{2}-10t⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨{t}^{2}+4,\\,\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}+7⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-7218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r=⟨t+1,\\,\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}-10t+7⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-122471" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},0⟩\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(2,3)$ and has velocity $⟨1,-1⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-122471" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},0⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\text{d}t,\\int 0\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t}+{C}_{1},{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨1,-1⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{1}+{C}_{1},{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-1⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-1.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{t},-1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (-1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{C}_{3},-t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(1)=⟨2,3⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+{C}_{3},-1+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,3⟩ & =⟨{C}_{3},-1+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=2$ and ${C}_{4}=4.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,4-t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-122471" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-122471" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-122471" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r(t)=⟨2+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,3t⟩"] ["math" "r(t)=⟨2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,4-t⟩"] ["math" "r(t)=⟨2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,5t-2⟩"] ["math" "r(t)=⟨2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,4-t⟩"] ["math" "r(t)=⟨2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,5t-2⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-122471" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r(t)=⟨2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,4-t⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351538" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨-{e}^{-t},\\,20{t}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(3,-2)$ and has velocity $⟨0,\\,1⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$r(t)=⟨${{field-1}}$,\\,{t}^{5}+t-2\\,⟩$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351538" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨-{e}^{-t},\\,20{t}^{3}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (-{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 20{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{-t}+{C}_{1},\\,5{t}^{4}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨0,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨{e}^{0}+{C}_{1},\\,5(0)^{4}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso, we must have ${C}_{1}=-1$ and ${C}_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨{e}^{-t}-1,\\,5{t}^{4}+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨{e}^{-t}-1,\\,5{t}^{4}+1⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int ({e}^{-t}-1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (5{t}^{4}+1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{-t}-t+{C}_{3},\\,{t}^{5}+t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨3,-2⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨-{e}^{0}-0+{C}_{3},\\,{0}^{5}+0+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,-2⟩ & =⟨-1+{C}_{3},\\,{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso, we must have ${C}_{3}=4$ and ${C}_{4}=-2.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨-{e}^{-t}-t+4,\\,{t}^{5}+t-2⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351538" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-351538" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351538" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-e^{-t}-t+4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-7221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨6t-2,4⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0,0)$ and has velocity $⟨1,\\,4⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-7221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨6t-2,4⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (6t-2)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-2t+{C}_{1},\\,4t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨1,4⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨3(1)^{2}-2(1)+{C}_{1},\\,4(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,4⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},4+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨3{t}^{2}-2t,\\,4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3{t}^{2}-2t,\\,4t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (3{t}^{2}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+{C}_{3},\\,2{t}^{2}+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(1)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨(1)^{3}-(1)^{2}+{C}_{3},\\,2(1)^{2}+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{3},2+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=0$ and ${C}_{4}=-2.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{2}-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-7221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-7221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-7221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r=⟨2{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{2}-t⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨{t}^{3}+4t,\\,{t}^{2}-t⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+4t,\\,{t}^{2}-2t+6⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨{t}^{3}-t,\\,2{t}^{2}-2t+6⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{2}-2⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-7221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{2}-2⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨48(2t+1)^{2},\\,-6⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(2,-1)$ and has velocity $⟨8,\\,5⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$r(t)=⟨${{field-1}}$,\\,-3{t}^{2}+5t-1\\,⟩$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨48(2t+1)^{2},\\,-6⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 48(2t+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (-6)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8(2t+1)^{3}+{C}_{1},\\,-6t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨8,5⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨8(1)^{3}+{C}_{1},\\,-6(0)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨8,5⟩ & =⟨8+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso, we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=5.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t)=⟨8(2t+1)^{3},\\,-6t+5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity vector to obtain the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨8(2t+1)^{3},\\,-6t+5⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 8(2t+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (-6t+5)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(2t+1)^{4}+{C}_{3},\\,-3{t}^{2}+5t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=(2,-1).$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨(1)^{4}+{C}_{3},\\,0+0+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-1⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{3},\\,{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso, we must have ${C}_{3}=1$ and ${C}_{4}=-1.$ Therefore, the position vector of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(2t+1)^{4}+1,\\,-3{t}^{2}+5t-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39c943c9961f855413034e41b42c53211ea1e8596f422aca7f3a0f5403e7b925"} {"question" "q-351517" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "q-351517" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(2t+1)^4+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d50338a9183bbfda19d20772ca2af8a2e3eeb3ed56e93fcba9a76ca5b7a2f51"} {"question" "e-625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\langle 2t, 3t^2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(1,2)$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/example-625.json" "file_sha256" "6ebefb988b62363875cde72b804ad00d2096acaa9e1fca8161146fdad8a46211"} {"question" "e-625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2t,3{t}^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{r} (0)= \\langle 1,2 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨(0)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,(0)^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=1$ and $C_2=2.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{2}+1,\\,{t}^{3}+2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/example-625.json" "file_sha256" "6ebefb988b62363875cde72b804ad00d2096acaa9e1fca8161146fdad8a46211"} {"question" "e-626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\langle \\cos t, \\sin t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position  $\\mathbf{r}=\\left\\langle 0,\\, 0\\right\\rangle$ when $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}2,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=\\pi.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/example-626.json" "file_sha256" "c15c6aa06aadb3ce7ea5141824b5e6f3fa5bb3ab68a09535a361d6ae894b399b"} {"question" "e-626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{r}\\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) = \\langle 0,0 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,0+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=-1$ and $C_2 = 0.$ Therefore, the position is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the position at time $t=\\pi$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -1,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0-1,\\,-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/example-626.json" "file_sha256" "c15c6aa06aadb3ce7ea5141824b5e6f3fa5bb3ab68a09535a361d6ae894b399b"} {"question" "e-627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\langle 2t, 1 \\rangle\\,,\\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position  $\\mathbf{r} = \\left\\langle -1,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle$ when $t=0,$ calculate the times at which the particle is at the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/example-627.json" "file_sha256" "3794434d5886ba1eee64a2d3d81ed95e9107bb11bb079fea5737b05028f7a928"} {"question" "e-627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle is at the origin when its position is $\\mathbf{r} = \\left< 0,0 \\right>.$ So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2t,1⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,t+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{r} (0)= \\langle -1, \\, 1 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨(0)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,(0)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,\\,1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=-1$ and $C_2=1.$ Therefore, the position is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{2}-1,\\,t+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is at the origin, we solve for the times at which the position is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}^{2}-1,\\,t+1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-1 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 1, \\\\ t+1 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t= -1,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is at the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/example-627.json" "file_sha256" "3794434d5886ba1eee64a2d3d81ed95e9107bb11bb079fea5737b05028f7a928"} {"question" "e-7343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}(t) =  \\left<   9\\cos {3t},\\, \\sin{t} \\right>.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0,0)$ and has velocity $\\left\\langle 2,\\,0  \\right\\rangle$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/example-7343.json" "file_sha256" "2fbebcda0e27299654c37cb3c5a699b73005b9687026e75fbd09c77b02813860"} {"question" "e-7343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{v} (0)= \\langle 2,\\, 0 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,\\,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},-1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=2$ and $C_2=1.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\left<      3\\sin{3t} +2 , \\,    1-\\cos{t} \\right>.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+2,\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+2\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+2t+{C}_{3},\\,t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{r} (0)= \\langle 0,0 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2(0)+{C}_{3},\\,0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨-1+{C}_{3},\\,{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_3=1$ and $C_4=0.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left<  -\\cos {3t} + 2t + 1 , \\,   t-\\sin{t}  \\right>.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064720/example-7343.json" "file_sha256" "2fbebcda0e27299654c37cb3c5a699b73005b9687026e75fbd09c77b02813860"}] "usage" {"e-626" [] "q-7221" [] "e-627" [] "e-7343" [] "e-625" []} "basis" 2123 "retractions" [[17592186079111 :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$a(t) = ⟨6t - 2, 4⟩$.\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0, 0)$ and has velocity $⟨1, 4⟩$ when $t = 1$, calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t$."] [17592186078252 :question/problem "The velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\langle 2t, 3t^2\\rangle$.\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(1,2)$ when $t=0$, calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t$."] [17592186078252 :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= ∫v(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫⟨2t, 3t^{2}⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨∫2tdt, ∫3t^{2}dt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨t^{2} + C_{1}, t^{3} + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} (0)= \\langle 1,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) &= ⟨(0)^{2} + C_{1}, (0)^{3} + C_{2}⟩ \\\\\n⟨1, 2⟩ &= ⟨C_{1}, C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=1$ and $C_2=2$. Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= ⟨t^{2} + 1, t^{3} + 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186078253 :question/problem "The velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\langle \\cos t, \\sin t\\rangle$.\nGiven that the particle is at the position $\\mathbf{r}=\\left\\langle 0,\\, 0\\right\\rangle$ when $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}2$, calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=\\pi$."] [17592186078253 :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= ∫v(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫⟨\\cos t, \\sin t⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨∫\\cos tdt, ∫\\sin tdt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\sin t + C_{1},-\\cos t + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) = \\langle 0,0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(\\frac{π}{2}) &= ⟨\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) + C_{1},-\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) + C_{2}⟩ \\\\\n⟨0, 0⟩ &= ⟨1 + C_{1}, 0 + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=-1$ and $C_2 = 0$. Therefore, the position is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= ⟨\\sin t - 1,-\\cos t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the position at time $t=\\pi$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(π) &= ⟨\\sin π - 1,-\\cos π⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0 - 1, - (-1)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186078254 :question/problem "The velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\langle 2t, 1 \\rangle\\,,\\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\nGiven that the particle is at the position $\\mathbf{r} = \\left\\langle -1,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle$ when $t=0$, calculate the times at which the particle is at the origin."] [17592186078254 :question/worked-solution "The particle is at the origin when its position is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\left< 0,0 \\right>\n$$\n\nSo first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= ∫v(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫⟨2t, 1⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨∫2tdt, ∫1dt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨t^{2} + C_{1}, t + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} (0)= \\langle -1, \\, 1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) &= ⟨(0)^{2} + C_{1}, (0) + C_{2}⟩ \\\\\n⟨ - 1, 1⟩ &= ⟨C_{1}, C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=-1$ and $C_2=1$. Therefore, the position is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= ⟨t^{2} - 1, t + 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is at the origin, we solve for the times at which the position is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= 0 \\\\\n⟨t^{2} - 1, t + 1⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} - 1 &= 0⇒t = ± 1, \\\\\nt + 1 &= 0⇒t =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t= -1$, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is at the origin."] [17592186078277 :question/problem "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\left< 9\\cos {3t},\\, \\sin{t} \\right>$.\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0,0)$ and has velocity $\\left\\langle 2,\\,0 \\right\\rangle$ when $t=0$, calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t$."] [17592186078277 :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫⟨9\\cos 3t, \\sin t⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨∫9\\cos 3tdt, ∫\\sin tdt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3\\sin 3t + C_{1},-\\cos t + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} (0)= \\langle 2,\\, 0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(0) &= ⟨3\\sin 0 + C_{1},-\\cos 0 + C_{2}⟩ \\\\\n⟨2, 0⟩ &= ⟨C_{1},-1 + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=2$ and $C_2=1$. Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\left< 3\\sin{3t} +2, \\, 1-\\cos{t} \\right>\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= ∫v(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫⟨3\\sin 3t + 2, 1 - \\cos t⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨∫3\\sin 3t + 2dt, ∫1 - \\cos tdt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - \\cos 3t + 2t + C_{3}, t - \\sin t + C_{4}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} (0)= \\langle 0,0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) &= ⟨ - \\cos 0 + 2(0) + C_{3}, 0 - \\sin 0 + C_{4}⟩ \\\\\n⟨0, 0⟩ &= ⟨ - 1 + C_{3}, C_{4}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_3=1$ and $C_4=0$. Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left< -\\cos {3t} + 2t + 1, \\, t-\\sin{t} \\right>\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186077878 17592186077879 17592186077880 17592186077881 17592186077882] "immutable_fields" [[17592186